The Bloody Sunday Inquiry was a public inquiry chaired by Lord Saville of Newdigate into the events of 30 January 1972 in Derry, Northern Ireland, when paratroopers of the 1st Battalion, Parachute Regiment (1 PARA) shot and killed 13 unarmed civilians and injured 14 others during a civil rights march organised by the Northern Ireland Civil Rights Association (NICRA).^{[1]}^{[2]}

Following the publication of the Widgery Tribunal's report in 1972, which exonerated the soldiers of wrongdoing, the families of the deceased campaigned for a new inquiry to be undertaken. In 1998, the Labour government of Tony Blair announced that there would be a new inquiry, which commenced in 1999. The inquiry was the longest-running judicial inquiry in British history, lasting 12 years and costing £195 million (equivalent to £250 million in 2015).^{[3]}

